Cellulose paper packaging materials have gained considerable attention as substitutes for petroleum-based plastics owing to their biodegradability, renewability, flexibility, and good mechanical strength. However, high hydrophilicity and the absence of essential antibacterial activity limit their application in food packaging. In this study, a facile and energy-saving method was developed to improve the hydrophobicity of cellulose paper and endow it with a long-acting antibacterial effect by integrating cellulose paper substrate with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). A dense and homogenous coating of regular hexagonal ZnMOF-74 nanorods was in-situ formed on a paper surface by layer-by-layer assembly followed by low-surface-energy polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) modification to prepare a superhydrophobic $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@\mathrm{paper}.$ Excellent anti-fouling, selfcleaning, and antibacterial adhesion performances were obtained for this superhydrophobic paper. In addition, active carvacrol was loaded into the pores of ZnMOF-74 nanorods on $\mathsf{P M S@(Z n M O F–74)}_{5}@\mathsf{p}$ aper to combine antibacterial adhesion together with bactericidal ability, ultimately resulting in a completely “bacteria-free” surface and sustained antibacterial performance. The resultant superhydrophobic papers not only showed overall migration values within the limit of $10\mathrm{\mg/dm^{2}}$ but also good stability against various harsh mechanical, environmental, and chemical treatments. This work gave insights into the potential of in-situ-developed MOFsdopped coating as a functionally modified platform for preparing active superhydrophobic paper-based packaging.
## 1. Introduction
The excessive use of petroleum-based, non-biodegradable plastic packaging materials poses a serious threat to the environment and the ecological system, which has motivated researchers into biodegradable and sustainable alternatives [1]. Cellulose is one of the most abundant biopolymers in nature, providing a sustainable green resource that is renewable, biodegradable, non-toxic, and cost-effective [2]. Tradition­ ally, cellulose papers are ubiquitous and well accepted in our daily lives for green food packaging applications and are be used in various pack­ aging forms, such as paper cups, bags, and boxes [3,4]. However, the inherent high hydrophilicity and absence of antibacterial effects of cellulose papers readily result in contaminant adhesion, bacterial erosion and reproduction, and even breakage of packaging during food processing, shipping, and storage, which severely restricts their appli­ cations [5–7]. Therefore, improving the hydrophobicity and antibacterial performance of food packaging papers is vital.
A surface with a water contact angle (WCA) ${>}150^{\circ}$ and a sliding angle (SA) smaller than $10^{\circ}$ is defined as superhydrophobic [8]. Over the past decades, superhydrophobic surfaces have been rapidly developed owing to their wide applications in daily life and industrial fields, such as self-cleaning [9], oil–water separation [10], anti-fogging [11], UV shielding [12], and antibacterial adhesion, and so on [13]. In particular, many previous studies have applied superhydrophobic coatings to reduce or eliminate liquid food residues in packaging containers, which is of great significance in alleviating the food crisis [14,15]. Zhang et al. developed a thermo-resistant edible superhydrophobic coating by using beeswax and coffee lignin that effectively decreased the residue of highviscosity liquid foods, such as honey and yoghourt [16]. Wang et al. prepared an environmentally friendly starch-based superhydrophobic coating by combining starch nanoparticles with polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) that exhibited excellent water-proofing, self-cleaning, and liquid food residue reduction [17]. With the constant exploration of natural superhydrophobic surfaces such as lotus leaves, rice leaves, and butterfly wings, the construction principle of the artificial super­ hydrophobic surfaces has been understood. It is typically based on combining rough hierarchical structures (micro/nanostructures) and low-surface-energy substances (e.g., fluorides and alkanes) [18]. Based on this basic principle, various strategies have been proposed to develop superhydrophobic cellulose paper-based materials, including dip coating [19], atom transfer radical polymerization [20], plasma treat­ ment [21], phase separation [22], spraying [23], supercritical solution rapid expansion technology [24], and others [25]. However, these methods have some disadvantages, such as the requirement of special­ ized and expensive instruments, use of toxic or high-cost chemicals, and relatively complex and energy-intensive operation processes. Therefore, the development of a facile and energy-saving method to fabricate superhydrophobic paper-based materials is highly significant.
Layer-by-layer (LBL) assembly, first introduced by Decher et al., has been proven to be a simple, inexpensive, and versatile approach to achieve a desired surface modification by depositing multilayers with tailored compositions and architectures on solid substates [26]. Our research group has developed a series of LBL assembly systems to pre­ pare superhydrophobic papers, mostly involving first achievement of appropriate roughness by using inorganic nanoparticles (such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ ZnO, montmorillonite, and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)) fol­ lowed by decoration with a low-surface-energy compound [27–30]. As an important class of two-dimensional nanoparticles, MOFs produced by the self-assembly of metal ions or clusters and organic ligands through metal-coordination bonds have recently received considerable attention in the food packaging field because of their high porosity, large specific surface area, antibacterial activity, and excellent polymer compatibility [31–33]. MOFs can be used as nanocarriers to encapsulate unstable bioactive compounds for their controlled release [34,35]. For instance, Zhao et al. prepared gelatin/chitosan antibacterial packaging films by adding of capsaicin-loaded FeIII-HMOF-5 nanoparticles [36]. Lashkari et al. used three different MOFs nanoparticles as novel delivery systems to encapsulate volatile allyl isothiocyanate (AITC) and achieved its controlled release under high relative humidity [37]. These pioneering studies motivated us to explore the development of active super­ hydrophobic paper-based materials doped with MOFs nanoparticles. It is speculated that appropriate roughness can be achieved by depositing of MOFs nanoparticles on a paper surface, which is essential for endowing superhydrophobicity. Moreover, bioactive compounds (such as essential oils) can be loaded into MOFs nanoparticles benefiting from their high specific surface area and accessible porous structure, which enable their sustained release [38]. Some studies have been reported the simple coating of MOFs nanoparticle-containing formulation on the cellulose fiber-based substrates (such as cotton fabric) for functional modification [39,40]. However, the poor interaction between MOFs nanoparticles and substrates remains an inevitable issue needing to be addressed. In addition, the synthetic methods (e.g., hydrothermal and solventthermal) of most MOFs nanoparticles required high-temperature and high-pressure experimental conditions, thereby necessitating a high energy consumption and a high production cost, which limits their large-scale applications. To overcome these issues, the integration of MOFs into paper surfaces by in-situ LBL growth under ambient condi­ tions via covalent bonding could is a feasible solution [41,42]. To our best knowledge, thus far, the development of active superhydrophobic paper-based food packaging doped with MOFs nanoparticles by in-situ growth has hardly been explored.
In this work, the LBL assembly technique was adopted to prepare superhydrophobic paper via in-situ growth of zinc-based MOF (ZnMOF74) under ambient conditions, followed by low-surface-energy modifi­ cation. Fluorine-free and non-toxic PDMS was chosen as the low-surfaceenergy substance. The changes in the surface morphology, chemical composition, crystalline pattern, and surface wettability of the papers after treatment were studied. Furthermore, utilizing the high porosity and large specific surface area of ZnMOF-74, carvacrol (CV), one of the most widely studied essential oil components with high antibacterial activity, was encapsulated into the resultant superhydrophobic paper to create active superhydrophobic paper. The antifouling, waterresistance, self-cleaning, and tensile strength properties of the pre­ pared superhydrophobic paper were evaluated. The antibacterial ac­ tivity and antibacterial adhesion performance of the plain superhydrophobic and active superhydrophobic papers were investi­ gated. The release behavior of CV from the active superhydrophobic paper was studied. The overall migration and durability of the obtained functional papers were measured.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Commercial filter papers were obtained from Fuyang Special Paper Co., Ltd. (Hangzhou, China). Zinc acetate hexahydrate (Zn $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and methylene orange were purchased from Xilong Scientific Co., Ltd. (Shantou, China), and 2, 5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (DHTA) and CV were acquired from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). PDMS Prepolymer (Sylgard 184A) and its solidifying agent (Sylgard 184B) were provided by Dow Corning (Midland, MI, USA). All of the chemicals were analytically pure and were used without any treatment.
## 2.2. Fabrication of superhydrophobic paper
Scheme 1 illustrates the preparation process of a superhydrophobic paper. Briefly, a commercial filter paper was dipped into a $0.2\mathrm{mol/L}$ of zinc acetate solution at room temperature for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ , subsequently washed three times with deionized water, and dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Following this, the treated filter paper was dipped into a $0.1\ \mathrm{mol/L}$ of DHTA solution at room temperature for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and the same washing and drying procedures as in the previous step were performed. These two steps were regarded as one cycle, which yield the treated paper referred to as $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{1}@\mathbb{I}$ paper. n cycles were conducted until the desired roughness of the paper was achieved, and this treated paper is referred to as $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{\mathrm{n}}@$ paper. Finally, the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{\mathrm{n}}@$ paper sample was immersed in an ethyl acetate solution containing $1\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ of Sylgard 184A and $0.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Sylgard 184B at room temperature for 5 min and then cured at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . The final treated paper is referred to as $\mathrm{PDMS@(ZnMOF–74)_{n}}@\mathfrak{p}$ aper.
## 2.3. Fabrication of active superhydrophobic paper
A resultant superhydrophobic paper $(\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@\mathrm{paper})$ was dipped into $1.0\mathrm{mg/mL}$ of CV solution (CV was dissolved in absolute ethanol) for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , removed, washed three times with absolute ethanol, and dried in air overnight to obtain a CV-loaded superhydrophobic paper. It is referred to as $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@\mathfrak{p}$ aper. The CV loading capacity was calculated as the amount of CV loaded per unit area of the paper sample. To determine the CV loading capacity, a $2\times2$ cm paper sample was immersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of absolute ethanol and incubated at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ with ultrasound-assistance, after which the supernatant was collected. Following this, fresh $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of absolute ethanol was re-added, and CV was further extracted at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The supernatant was collected from two extractions, and absorbance at $276\ \mathrm{nm}$ was measured using a T90 UV–Visible (UV–Vis) spectropho­ tometer (Beijing Purkinje General Instrument Co., Ltd., Beijing, China). The amount of CV loaded in the active superhydrophobic paper was calculated from the standard curve of CV $(\mathbf{y}=14.44\times\mathit{\mathrm{~-~}}0.008$ , ${\mathrm{R}}^{2}=$ 0.998). All the measurements were performed in triplicate.
## 2.4. Characterization
The surface wettability of each paper was analyzed using an optical contact angle meter (JY-PHa, Chengde Jinhe Instrument Manufacturing Co., Ltd., Chengde, China) to record the surface water contact angle (WCA). The surface chemical composition of each paper was investi­ gated using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Thermo ESCALAB 250Xi, Axis Ultra DLD Kratos AXIS SUPRA, USA) with Al Kα X-ray ra­ diation $\mathrm{(h\nu=1486.6~eV)}$ ). The surface chemical groups of each paper were analyzed using attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) spectroscopy (Nicolet iS 10, Thermo Fisher Scien­ tific, USA) with a wavenumber range from 4000 to $500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and a spectral resolution of $4~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The surface morphology and elemental distribution of each paper were analyzed using a scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Zeiss Gemini 300, Germany) equipped with energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS). The crystalline pattern of each paper was recorded using X-ray diffraction (XRD) (Bruker D8 Advance, Germany) at scanning angles (2θ) from $5^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ with $\mathtt{C u K\alpha}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54{\dot{\mathrm{\AA}}})$ at $40\mathrm{kV},$ . The tensile strength of each paper was measured using a tensile tester (QLW-5E, Qunlong Instrument Co., Ltd., Xiamen, China) at $23^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $50\%$ relative humidity.
## 2.5. Antibacterial testing
Three different modified paper samples including (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper, $\mathrm{PDMS@(ZnMOF–74)_{5}@p a p e r}_{\leftmoon}$ , and $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF-}$ $74)_{5}@$ paper were chosen to evaluate their antibacterial activities against Gram-positive bacterium (Staphylococcus aureus, S. aureus) and Gram-negative bacterium (Escherichia coli, E. coli) by zone inhibition tests. The tested paper samples were first cut into discs with a diameter of approximately $6~\mathrm{mm}$ and sterilized using UV radiation for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Following this, a $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of bacterial suspension $({\sim}10^{6}~\mathrm{CFU/mL})$ was placed onto a Luria broth (LB) agar plate and spread evenly over each surface. Subsequently, a paper disc was stuck on the LB agar plate and then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for certain periods (24, 48, and $72\mathrm{{h}}$ ). The width of the inhibition zone was measured using a Vernier caliper and calcu­ lated using the Eq. (1):
$$
{\mathrm{Width~of~inhibition~zone}}={\frac{d_{1}-d_{2}}{2}}
$$
where $d_{1}$ is the total diameter of the sample and the inhibition zone, $d_{2}$ is the diameter of the sample $\left(6\mathrm{mm}\right)$ .
## 2.6. Antibacterial adhesion testing
E. coli and S. aureus were chosen as the model bacteria to assess the antibacterial adhesion capability of the resultant superhydrophobic papers. The testing process was performed according to our previously reported method [43]. For comparison, an original paper and a (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper sample were used. Each sample was measured in triplicate, and the mean values were calculated.
## 2.7. Release of CV from active superhydrophobic paper
The release of the loaded CV from an active superhydrophobic paper into food was determined using different food simulants, including $10\%$ $(\nu/\nu)$ and $95~\%$ (v/v) ethanol aqueous solutions, which represent alco­ holic and high-fat foods, respectively. Three pieces of active super­ hydrophobic paper samples $(1\times1~\mathrm{cm})$ were placed in a testing tube containing ${5}\mathrm{mL}$ of food simulant and shaken at $120\mathrm{rpm}$ for a set period of time under ambient conditions. At specific time intervals, $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of solution were removed, and the absorbance at $276\ \mathrm{nm}$ was recorded using the UV–Vis spectrometer. The amount of CV released was deter­ mined using the standard curve of CV, and the release rate of CV from active superhydrophobic paper was calculated. Each measurement was performed in triplicate, and the mean values were determined.
## 2.8. Overall migration testing
The overall migration of the components of $\mathrm{PDMS@(ZnMOF\mathrm{.}}$ $74)_{5}@$ paper and $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper into the $10\%$ (v/v) ethanol and $95\ \%$ (v/v) ethanol food simulants was analyzed according to the method of Moreno et al. with slight modifications [44]. Briefly, a $2\times3$ cm paper sample was immersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of simulant and incu­ bated for 10 days at $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Subsequently, the paper sample was taken out from the simulant, the simulant was evaporated, and the residue was weighed after drying at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The rate of overall migration was expressed in $\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{dm}^{2}$ . Each sample was analyzed in triplicate, and the average values were calculated.
## 2.9. Durability testing
The durability testing of $\mathrm{PDMS@(ZnMOF–74)_{5}@p a}$ per and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{PDMS}({\underline{{a}}})$ $(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}-74)_{5}@1$ paper samples comprised three parts: mechanical, environmental, and chemical stability. (1) Mechanical stability was evaluated by sandpaper abrasion, bending, finger-wiping, and tapepeeling. (2) Environmental stability was evaluated by keeping the paper samples under $76\%$ relative humidity and at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . (3) Chemical stability was evaluated by immersing the paper samples in aqueous solutions with different pH values, 2 M NaCl aqueous solution, and three types of organic solvents at room temper­ ature for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the aforementioned treatment, the WCAs of the paper samples were measured.
## 2.10. Statistical analysis
All data were presented as mean $\pm$ standard deviation (SD) using SPSS software (version 26.0, IBM Corp., Armonk, USA) by one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) and Duncan's multiple range test at $P<$ 0.05.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of (ZnMOF-74)n@paper and PDMS@(ZnMOF74)n@paper
The surface morphologies of paper samples after the in-situ growth of ZnMOF-74 by LBL assembly were observed by SEM. As shown in Fig. 1A, the surface of the original paper is relatively flat and clean. As the original paper is alternatively dipped into zinc ion- and DHTA ligandcontaining solutions for several cycles, rod-like crystals of ZnMOF-74 are observed on its surface. Moreover, the amount and size of the ZnMOF-74 increase with an increasing number of cycles (as shown in Fig. 1B–E). When the number of cycles exceeds five, a homogenous and dense coating of ZnMOF-74 crystals almost completely covers on the paper surface, exhibiting regular hexagonal nanorods, which is in agreement with the findings of Sui et al. [45]. During the in-situ growth process of ZnMOF-74, the abundant hydroxyl groups on the original paper serve not only as binding sites for Zn ions but also as nucleation sites for the continuous growth of ZnMOF-74 nanoparticles. This pro­ vides a simple route to get an appropriate rough structure on a paper surface for further treatment. To achieve superhydrophobic modifica­ tion, $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{\mathrm{n}}@$ paper samples were further treated with lowsurface-energy PDMS. The SEM image of a PDMS $@$ (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper sample is shown in Fig. 1F. A dense and uniform coating is observed, similar to the morphology of a $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{5}@$ paper sample, indicating that the surface morphology of ZnMOF-74 crystals is insig­ nificantly influenced by the PDMS treatment. Additionally, the surface elemental composition and distribution of a PDMS $@$ (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper sample were analyzed using SEM-EDS. As shown in in Fig. 1G and H, four elements including C, O, Zn, and Si are detected. Among them, Si is only attributed to PDMS, and Zn is derived from Zn $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}$ . The EDS mapping of the $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\ –74)_{5}@\mathrm{J}$ aper sample exhibits an even elemental distribution on its surface, demon­ strating a uniform dispersion of ZnMOF-74 and PDMS.
The surface hydrophobicity of each $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{\mathrm{n}}@\mathrm{paper}$ sample was evaluated using WCA, and the results are shown in Fig. 2A. The WCAs for the PDMS $@$ paper, $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\ –\74)_{1}@$ paper, $\mathrm{PDMS}@$ $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{3}@\mathfrak{p}$ aper, $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@\mathrm{paper}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{PDMS}({\underline{{a}}})$ (ZnMOF-74)7 $@$ paper samples are $118.2^{\circ}$ , $126.8^{\circ}$ , $136.5^{\circ}$ , $156.9^{\circ}$ , and $157.7^{\circ}$ , respectively. These results are attributed to the synergistic effect of the appropriate rough structure achieved by the ZnMOF-74 crystal deposition and the PDMS treatment. Superhydrophobic PDMS $@$ $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper samples were chosen for further study.
The surface chemical groups of different papers were analyzed using FTIR, and the results are shown in Fig. 2B. Characteristic cellulose ab­ sorption peaks are observed in the spectrum of original paper. These include the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching vibration at $3331~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ stretching and bending vibrations at 2966 and $1428~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively, and C–O vibration at $1029~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . In the spectrum of a $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper sample, a wide absorption band observed in the range of 3500–3000 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ is attributed to $-\mathrm{oH}$ stretching vibration of the MOF ligands as well as the absorbed water on the MOF surface. The two peaks at 1552 and $1408\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to the symmetric and asymmetric vibrations of –COO in the ligands, respectively. The peak at $1194\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is probably attributed to the $\mathtt{C-O}$ vibration of the organic ligand DHTA. The peaks at 883 and $815~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-O}}{\mathsf{-Z}}{\mathsf{n}}$ vibrations of ZnMOF74 [46]. The peak at $584~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ vibration of the benzene ring of the organic ligand DHTA. These peaks are expected from the literature and indicate the successful in-situ growth of ZnMOF-74 in this study. Two new peaks are observed at 2966 and $1024~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the spectrum of a $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}.74)_{5}@\mathfrak{p}$ aper sample compared with those in the spectrum of the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper sample. These are attributed to the ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ and $s\mathrm{i}{-}0{-}s\mathrm{i}$ vibrations of PDMS, demon­ strating the formation of a PDMS decorating layer on the (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper sample [43].
To further explore the presence of PDMS and ZnMOF-74 nano­ particles on the surfaces of the obtained papers, XPS analyses were conducted (Fig. 2C). The original paper, $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{5}@\mathrm{I}$ paper, and $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\ –74)_{5}@1$ paper sample spectra display the same binding energies at 284.1 and $530.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which are attributed to C 1s and O 1s, respectively. Compared with the XPS spectrum of the original paper, that of the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{5}@$ paper shows three new peaks at 88.4, 1046.7, and $1023.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which are attributed to the $\mathtt{Z n3p}$ , satellite peaks asso­ ciated with Zn 2p1/2 and $\mathtt{Z n2p3/2}$ , respectively [28]. This observation is in accordance with the ZnMOF-74 structure. After decoration with PDMS, the peak intensities of $\mathrm{Zn}\ 2\mathrm{p}1/2$ and Zn 2p3/2 decreased significantly, and two new remarkable peaks belonging to Si 2p (102.3 eV) and Si 2s (152.0 eV) are detected. This further supports the forma­ tion of a PDMS layer on the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{5}@$ paper surface.
The XRD patterns of original paper, $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{5}@\mathrm{f}$ aper, and $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@\mathrm{paper}$ samples are displayed in Fig. 2D. Diffraction peaks at $14.9^{\circ}$ , $16.1^{\circ}$ and $22.4^{\circ}$ , attributed to the (1–10), (110), and (200) crystalline planes of the cellulose I structure, respec­ tively, are observed in the XRD pattern of the original paper [43]. Two characteristic peaks at $6.8^{\circ}$ and $11.6^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (110) and (300) planes of the ZnMOF-74, respectively, are detected in the XRD pattern of the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper sample [46]. These confirm the successful in-situ synthesis of ZnMOF-74 nanoparticles on the paper surface. Moreover, the XRD pattern of the PDMS $\ @(\mathrm{ZnMOF}-74)_{5}@\mathrm{pape}$ r sample is similar to that of the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot\mathrm{7}4)_{5}@$ paper sample, revealing that the crystal structure of ZnMOF-74 does not change after the PDMS treatment. Comprehensively considering the SEM, FTIR, XPS, XRD, and WCA results, we concluded that a superhydrophobic paper was fabri­ cated by combining the in-situ growth of ZnMOF-74 nanoparticles using the LBL method on a paper surface with a low-surface-energy PDMS treatment.
## 3.2. Anti-fouling, water-resistance, self-cleaning, and mechanical strength
## 3.3. Antibacterial activity
As shown in Fig. 4A and C, the original paper exhibits no antibac­ terial activity against S. aureus and E. coli without any inhibition zone as expected. In contrast, the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper, PDMS $@$ (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper, and PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF-74) $_{15}@$ paper samples present remarkable antibacterial activities against S. aureus and $E$ . coli with distinct inhibition zones. As plotted in Fig. 4B and C, in the first $24\mathrm{h}$ , the widths of the inhibition zones of the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper sample against S. aureus and E. coli are $1.40\mathrm{mm}$ and $0.30\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively. The widths of the inhibition zones of the $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\ –\ Z4)_{5}@_{}$ paper sample against S. aureus and E. coli are1.00 and $0.15\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively. Therefore, the antibacterial activity of the $(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@$ paper sample is better than that of the PDMS $\underline{{\widehat{a}}}(\mathrm{ZnMOF}{-}74)_{5}\underline{{\widehat{a}}}$ paper sample, which is attributed to the easy instability of ZnMOF-74 in water or humidity. This results in the dissolution out of antibacterial substances $(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+})$ , whereas the post-treatment with hydrophobic PDMS improves the water stability of ZnMOF-74 and thus leads to a relatively small amount of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ release. In addition, the $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@\mathfrak{p c}$ per sample shows the strongest antibacterial activities against S. aureus and E. coli, with widths of the inhibition zones against S. aureus and E. coli of $7.10\mathrm{mm}$ and 3.95 mm in the first $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . This indicates that CV plays a major part in the antibacterial effect. Furthermore, the inhibition zone widths are observed to insignificantly decrease with increasing time compared to those in the first $24\ \mathrm{~h~}$ . In particular, the $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF-}$ $74)_{5}@$ paper sample exhibits superior and long-acting antibacterial ac­ tivity to the other papers, which can be probably attributed to the sus­ tainable release of CV from the $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}-74)_{5}@|$ paper sample to inhibit bacterial growth. The release behavior of CV from a $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@$ paper sample is further discussed in the following section.
## 3.4. Synergistic effect of antibacterial adhesion and bactericidal ability
Bacterial contamination of food packaging during packaging, ship­ ping, and storage processes accelerates food spoilage, and thus, shortens their shelf-life. Bacterial adhesion to a material is the first step in bacterial colonization and the subsequent biofilm formation, thus, inhibiting the initial adhesion of bacteria to a material is significant [47]. It is well known that a superhydrophobic surface can decrease bacterial adhesion owing to its low surface energy and trapped air pockets [48]. The antibacterial adhesion abilities of original and superhydrophobic paper samples against S. aureus and $E$ . coli were evaluated using the plate colony counting method. As shown in Fig. 5B and C, the amount of adhered bacteria on the original paper is signifi­ cantly greater than that on the superhydrophobic PDMS $@$ (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper sample. The bacterial adhesion amount of the $\mathrm{PDMS}@$ (ZnMOF-74) $\scriptstyle5@$ paper sample is reduced by $87.5~\%$ and $88.4~\%$ against S. aureus and E. coli, respectively, compared to that of the original paper. This finding proves the excellent antibacterial adhesion performance of the as-prepared superhydrophobic paper. Unfortunately, a small num­ ber of live bacteria are still firmly attached on the superhydrophobic paper surface. These adhered bacteria form biofilms on super­ hydrophobic paper surface and subsequently colonize and multiply, ultimately resulting in a bacterial-polluted paper surface and a loss of superhydrophilicity. Thus, if a superhydrophobic packaging material surface contains a bactericidal substance that can be slowly released to the surrounding environment, any residual attached bacteria, even the bacteria around the packaging inner environment, will be killed by the bactericidal substance. This is highly important to retard food spoilage and extend shelf-life. As illustrated in Fig. 5A, the hydrophilic (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper releases antibacterial $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions to some extent because of the water instability of ZnMOF-74, but a large number of live bacteria still adhere and form stable biofilms on the paper sample surface owing to its high hydrophilicity. In contrast, a completely “bacteria-free” sur­ face is successfully achieved for the $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@\mathrm{p}$ aper sample. This finding can be explained by the following two reasons, as shown in Fig. 5A. The first one is the repulsion of most bacteria based on the antibacterial adhesion property of the superhydrophobic surface. The second reason is the release of bactericidal substances, including $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and CV, resulting in the killing of the bacteria. Here, we should point that even though maybe some bacteria adhere onto the paper surface, the bactericidal substances $(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ and CV) can effectively damage the adhered bacterial structure and kill them, thereby forming unstable biofilms of dead bacteria. These dead bacteria are easily removed from the paper surface under the impact of running water or an external force, which does not influence the surface morphology of the paper sample and its superhydrophobicity [49]. Overall, the as-prepared $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@$ paper showed a synergistic effect of anti­ bacterial adhesion and bactericidal ability, which is promising for practical applications in food packaging.
## 3.5. Release behavior of CV from PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF-74)5@paper
ZnMOF-74 nanorods have high porosity that can encapsulate CV to some extent, and the abundant hydroxyl groups in their ligands provide numerous adsorption sites for CV via hydrogen bonding interactions. Therefore, an active superhydrophobic paper was fabricated. The release behavior of essential oils directly affects the antibacterial actions of active packaging materials. In this study, the release behavior of CV from $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}-74)_{5}@.$ paper was measured using $10\%(\nu/\nu)$ and $95\ \%$ (v/v) ethanol solutions serving as alcoholic and fatty food simulants, respectively. As shown in Fig. 6A, a similar release trend occurred in both food simulants. Two stages are involved in the release of CV: the fast release of CV during the early $24\mathrm{h}$ and the slow release in the late period. Ethanol molecules first penetrate into the internal structure of the paper sample, access the pores of ZnMOF-74, and dissolve CV. The dissolved CV molecules diffuse from the interior of the paper sample to the simulant solution until thermodynamic equilibrium is achieved. Moreover, it is worth noting that the release rate of CV in 10 $\%$ ethanol (alcoholic food simulant) is higher than that in $95\%$ ethanol (fatty food simulant), which is related to the polarity of the simulants. The high polar solvent ( $10\%$ ethanol) promotes damage to the hydrogen bonding between ZnMOF-74 and CV, thereby helping the escape of CV from the active paper. These results demonstrate that CV is encapsulated into the porous structure of ZnMOF-74 nanorods, endowing a sustained and controlled release effect to the paper-based packaging materials, thereby prolonging the working duration of the active ingredients.
## 3.6. Overall migration
A packaging material should have minimal interactions with the food. The material used for packaging should not release additives into the food in unacceptable amounts [50]. The overall migration of the components of the $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\cdot74)_{5}@\mathrm{paper}$ and $\mathrm{PDMS}@$ $(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}-74)_{5}@\mathrm{F}$ aper samples into the tested food simulants $10\%$ ethanol for alcoholic foods, and $95\ \%$ ethanol for fatty foods) was determined. The results are shown in Fig. 6B. The overall migration values of the $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\ –\74)_{5}@$ paper and $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF-}$ $74)_{5}@$ paper samples into $95~\%$ ethanol are less than those into $10~\%$ ethanol, which is due to that the high polarity of the food simulant fa­ cilitates its penetration into the superhydrophobic coating, thus causing interaction with components. The overall migration values of the $\mathrm{PDMS@(ZnMOF–74)_{5}@p a p e r}$ and $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@\mathrm{p}\mathsf{z}$ per samples are below than the permissible limit $(10\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{dm}^{2})$ for the tested simulants, indicating their compatibility and safety for food packaging applications.
## 3.7. Durability
Whether superhydrophobic food packaging materials maintain longterm stability or are destroyed during food processing, transportation, and storage is a criterion for evaluating their usage values in practical applications. Superhydrophobic materials easily lose their super­ hydrophobicity under rigorous conditions, such as mechanical wear, extreme environments, and corrosive solutions. Hence, the mechanical, environmental, and chemical stabilities of the PDMS $@$ (ZnMOF$74)_{5}@$ paper and $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}.74)_{5}@\mathrm{F}$ aper samples were thoroughly assessed. As shown in the inset of Fig. 7A, each tested paper sample is placed face down on sandpaper $(1200~\mathrm{{Cw})}$ with a weight loading of $50~\mathrm{g}$ , and moved $10\mathrm{cm}$ back and forth along a ruler in a cycle. After eight cycles of abrasion, the WCA of PDMS $\underline{{\widehat{a}}}(\mathrm{ZnMOF}{\cdot}74)_{5}@\mathrm{pa}\mathrm{_{F}}$ er decline from $156.9^{\circ}$ to $150^{\circ}$ , and that of $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{CV}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}.$ $74)_{5}@$ paper decreases from $157.2^{\circ}$ to $150.1^{\circ}$ , indicating their super­ hydrophobicity is maintained. Nevertheless, ten cycles of abrasion could slightly destroy the micro/nanostructure of the paper surface, resulting in a decreased WCA of $147.1^{\circ}$ . As shown in Fig. S2A, the surface microstructure of the paper sample after ten abrasion cycles is destroyed by the external abrasion force, which is the main cause of the decreased WCA. In addition, the bending, finger-wiping and tape-peeling tests were also conducted to simulate the mechanical influence in real-life applications. As shown in Fig. 7B–D, the surface WCAs of the paper samples still remain superhydrophobic after 100 bending backward and forward, 90 finger-wiping, and 40 tape-peeling cycles. The corre­ sponding surface microstructures displayed in Fig. S2B–D also exhibit some degree of damage after various mechanical treatments. These re­ sults indicate their excellent mechanical stability. Moreover, we placed paper samples under the following environmental conditions: a high temperature of $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , low temperature of $-20\ {}^{\circ}{\bf C}.$ , and $76\%$ relative humidity for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As shown in Fig. 7E, the WCAs of the $\mathrm{PDMS}@$ $(\mathrm{ZnMOF-}74)_{5}@\mathrm{pa}_{\mathrm{I}}$ er and $\mathrm{PDMS@(CV@ZnMOF–74)}_{5}@\mathrm{p}$ aper samples are still higher than $152^{\circ}$ , showing maintenance of superhydrophobicity and their excellent environmental stability. In addition, the paper samples were immersed in various corrosive solutions to further eval­ uate their chemical stability. As exhibited in Fig. 7F–G, the WCAs of the paper samples do not change significantly after immersion in acidic, neutral, and alkaline aqueous solutions, $2\mathrm{MNaCl}$ aqueous solution, and three types of organic solvents for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ . The WCAs of the paper samples are still ${>}150^{\circ}$ , indicating their excellent chemical stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, we developed a facile and energy-saving method to prepare a superhydrophobic paper by combining the in-situ growth of ZnMOF-74 nanorods with a low-surface-energy modification using PDMS. Superhydrophobicity of a paper substrate was successfully ach­ ieved by simply controlling the number of cycles of the LBL assembly. The $\mathrm{PDMS}@(\mathrm{ZnMOF}\ –\ q)_{5}@\$ paper showed a WCA of $156.9^{\circ}$ and dis­ played excellent anti-fouling, self-cleaning, and anti-bacterial adhesion performances. To further endow sustained antibacterial activity to superhydrophobic paper, active CV was loaded into the pore structures of ZnMOF-74 crystals and an active superhydrophobic paper with a completely “bacteria-free” surface was created. The overall migration value of the obtained active superhydrophobic paper was below the threshold limit of $10~\mathrm{\mg/dm^{\bar{2}}}$ , and the superhydrophobic paper demonstrated a good stability against harsh mechanical, environmental, and chemical treatments. Overall, the integration of paper-based ma­ terials with MOFs nanoparticles to prepare an active superhydrophobic paper provides a new insight to extend the application of paper-based materials in food packaging.
